    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Linear Equations and Models</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
             Recall the definition of the slope intercept form of a linear function.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                
            </p>

            <example workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the equation of a line with slope <m>-\frac{5}{7}</m> and <m>y</m>-intercept <m>4</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="a">
                <p>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
            </example>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equation of a line with slope <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> that goes through the point <m>(4, -1)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            The fact that we needed to solve for <m>b</m> or find the y-intercept in order to get the equation was a bit inconvenient. This leads to a new way of writing an equation for a line: <term>point-slope form</term>.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            The fact that we needed to solve for <m>b</m> or find the y-intercept in order to get the equation was a bit inconvenient. This leads to a new way of writing an equation for a line: <term>point-slope form</term>. This form of the equation is <md>
                y-y_0 = m(x-x_0)
            </md> for the line with slope <m>m</m> and going through the point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>.
            
        </p>
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equation of a line with slope <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> that goes through the point <m>(4, -1)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="a">
                <p>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equation of the line that goes through the poins <m>(-1, 4)</m> and <m>(5, -6)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        <solution component="a">
            <p>
                
